pouch boundary-line patch 0044 - the memory seam made exact (B-1b).

B-1b of the browser arc (ARCHITECTURE.md sec 6.5 "The permission ceiling",
"Range detach", "Capacity"; docs/browser-status.md "The B-1 decisions"). The
bar it serves is the operator's: a program is never refused memory while free
memory exists, and memory it relinquishes returns to the system so its
footprint shrinks at runtime -- on the Pouch substrate as on the native one.
Until this patch pouch's mman/ lower half was the P6-pouch-mem shape (0003):
mmap ignored prot and always minted RW, MAP_FIXED was refused, and mprotect,
madvise and mremap sat at the ENOSYS sentinel -- tolerated by mallocng by
construction (`&& errno != ENOSYS`), and the reason a pthread's guard was RW,
mallocng's freed slots inside a retained group kept their pages, and a
JavaScript engine's decommit returned nothing. Eight files:

  src/internal/_pouch_mman.h  NEW. The four Thylacine numbers the seam calls
      (SYS_thyla_burrow_reserve 124 / _protect 125 / _decommit 84 / _detach
      38) in the 0019 SYS_thyla_* self-contained idiom, the SEAL flag and the
      two auxv tags of 0045. NOT aliases of the Linux __NR_ names: three of
      the four differ from their namesakes in argument SHAPE (see the header),
      so the Linux names stay at the sentinel and a raw syscall(SYS_mprotect,
      ...) from a port is a clean ENOSYS, never a misread register.

  arch/aarch64/bits/syscall.h.in  __NR_mmap goes BACK to the sentinel. 0003
      aliased it to 83 (SYS_BURROW_ATTACH_LAZY, natively one argument: the
      length in x0); musl's one raw six-argument caller, src/env/__init_tls.c,
      reached it in Linux's shape, which the kernel's 83 arm has read since
      Clade CL-4 -- anonymous-private only, and RW whatever prot was asked.
      Now that the mapper's prot is exact, a raw Linux-shaped mmap must not
      reach a number that ignores its prot: parked, it is a clean ENOSYS, and
      0046 routes __init_tls through __mmap (REQUIRED by this parking).
      __NR_munmap stays 38: the range detach IS Linux's (addr, len). After a
      musl re-vendor, 0001's awk filter loses m["mmap"] (0003 added it) and
      keeps m["munmap"]="38".

  src/mman/mmap.c  Rewritten on 0003's text. The anonymous subset is EXACT:
      SYS_BURROW_RESERVE at the prot asked for under a mint-time ceiling of
      RW (PROT_NONE mints a reservation that faults until raised; PROT_READ
      read-only; W implies R as on AArch64 Linux; X is EACCES -- I-12 is
      never degraded). MAP_FIXED over one's own anonymous mapping is Linux's
      effect -- fresh zero pages at the new prot -- as SYS_BURROW_DECOMMIT
      then SYS_BURROW_PROTECT; it never creates a mapping at an address, and
      a range not wholly plain anonymous memory of this process is ENOMEM
      (WebKit's OSAllocator places its guard pages this way). The two calls
      are not one: a refusal of the second -- a range sealed below the prot
      asked (EACCES), no VMA slot left for a middle cut (ENOMEM) -- comes
      after the discard and leaves zero pages at the old prot; an atomic
      discard-and-protect needs a kernel flag (tracked).
      MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE: ENOSYS. File-backed: ENOSYS, as before.

  src/mman/munmap.c  Comment refresh (the range form since B-1a': partial
      and multi-mapping unmaps, holes answer 0) and Linux's two shape errors
      answered before the call (EINVAL for len 0 / an unaligned start).

  src/mman/madvise.c  MADV_DONTNEED / MADV_FREE -> SYS_BURROW_DECOMMIT (the
      pages go, the mapping stays, a later touch re-faults zero; the flat -1
      of a refused range is ENOMEM). The pure hints answer 0 without a
      syscall (Thylacine has no readahead / THP / dump policy to steer; a
      hint on an unmapped range is 0 here, ENOMEM on Linux -- documented).
      Any other advice: EINVAL.

  src/mman/mprotect.c  -> SYS_BURROW_PROTECT with FOUR arguments (the
      flags word, 0: the three-argument seam form would pass x3 as flags and
      a stray 1 would SEAL the range). Linux's normalisation (a bit beyond
      R|W|X is EINVAL; W implies R; a range that rounds to nothing is 0),
      then the kernel's -errno through: EACCES for X, for a raise past the
      ceiling, for a guard / shared / JIT mapping; ENOMEM for a hole.

  src/mman/posix_madvise.c  Through __madvise (a hint is 0, not the raw
      sentinel's ENOSYS); POSIX's advisory DONTNEED keeps its short-circuit.

  src/malloc/mallocng/glue.h  USE_MADV_FREE 1 (upstream ships 0): free() of
      a slot inside a RETAINED group madvise(MADV_FREE)s the whole pages
      inside it (free.c), so the footprint falls without the group being
      unmapped. A MADV_FREE page is one Linux may take at any moment, and
      mallocng rewrites a slot's header at its next enframe and never reads
      a freed slot's pages, so the eager release is inside the allocator's
      contract. THE SUBSTRATE WITNESS: pouch-hello-mem's mallocng-trim leg
      (the data-view page count of /proc/<pid>/status falls after every
      other block of a retained class is freed) is RED on a libc built with
      this line at 0.

What this makes real without a musl change: pthread_create's guard
(PROT_NONE mint + mprotect RW of the usable part -- src/thread/
pthread_create.c:294-300, untouched) -- pouch-hello-guard proves the fault;
mallocng's PROT_NONE metadata pages (malloc.c:82-95).

Census of raw SYS_mmap / SYS_munmap / SYS_mprotect / SYS_madvise callers in
musl src (control: src/mman/*.c must appear, and did): __init_tls.c (0046,
required by the parking above), posix_madvise.c (here), mremap.c (stays the
sentinel: realloc's copy fallback), the per-arch __unmapself (0004's aarch64
retarget: one detach over the whole stack mapping, now a two-piece range --
guard + body -- the range form serves).

Binding design: docs/POUCH-DESIGN.md sec 8.1, 8.2 (as built at B-1b);
ARCHITECTURE.md sec 6.5. Invariants P-1 (only Thylacine numbers cross the
seam), P-3 (every surface a defined behaviour or a documented errno), P-4
(mman/ and the mallocng OS glue are lower half). Kernel-side pins:
kernel/include/thylacine/syscall.h (124 / 125 / 84 / 38, BURROW_PROT_* ==
PROT_*).

--- a/src/internal/_pouch_mman.h
+++ b/src/internal/_pouch_mman.h
@@ -0,0 +1,33 @@
+#ifndef _POUCH_MMAN_H
+#define _POUCH_MMAN_H
+
+/* pouch boundary-line memory seam (B-1b; ARCHITECTURE.md sec 6.5 "The
+ * permission ceiling", "Range detach", "Capacity"). The Thylacine kernel
+ * syscall numbers the mman/ lower half calls, in the 0019 SYS_thyla_*
+ * self-contained idiom -- NOT aliases of the Linux __NR_ names, because three
+ * of the four differ from their Linux namesakes in argument SHAPE: reserve
+ * takes (length, prot, align_log2) and returns the VA; protect takes a FOURTH
+ * word, the flags, whose bit 0 SEALS the range; decommit takes (vaddr, length)
+ * only and would read a raw madvise's advice as nothing. The Linux names stay
+ * at the unimplemented sentinel, so a raw syscall(SYS_mprotect, ...) from a
+ * port is a clean ENOSYS, never a misread register (patch 0046 re-routes
+ * musl's one raw caller, __init_tls, which the parking would have broken).
+ *
+ * Mirrors of kernel/include/thylacine/syscall.h and elf.h. The pouch-hello-mem
+ * and pouch-hello-threads boot provers pin them by BEHAVIOUR against the
+ * kernel (a mint at PROT_NONE faults, a guard row appears in maps, the auxv
+ * pair equals the kernel's own `stack` row). */
+#define SYS_thyla_burrow_reserve   124   /* (length, prot, align_log2) -> vaddr | -errno */
+#define SYS_thyla_burrow_protect   125   /* (vaddr, length, prot, flags) -> 0 | -errno */
+#define SYS_thyla_burrow_decommit   84   /* (vaddr, length) -> 0 | -1 */
+#define SYS_thyla_burrow_detach     38   /* (vaddr, length) -> 0 | -1; = __NR_munmap */
+
+/* The prot word is Linux's PROT_* (the kernel pins BURROW_PROT_* to them). */
+#define POUCH_BURROW_PROTECT_SEAL   1u
+
+/* The auxiliary-vector tags the kernel writes for the initial thread's stack
+ * (elf.h AT_STACK_BASE / AT_STACK_SIZE; private tags above musl's AUX_CNT). */
+#define AT_THYLA_STACK_BASE  0x5342
+#define AT_THYLA_STACK_SIZE  0x5353
+
+#endif
